        <div id="gold-standard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Gold Standard 100% Whey Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard's protein comes from a blend of whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate and whey peptides, with isolate listed first, per the label at the time of writing. ON calls isolate the primary source. That means it is an isolate-led blend rather than a pure isolate: a little more lactose and fat than an isolate, but noticeably leaner than the concentrate-led budget wheys that dominate the cheap end of the market.
          </p>

          <p>
            The macros are lean. One scoop gives 24 g of protein in about 120 to 130 calories depending on flavor, typically with 1 to 1.5 g of fat and 3 to 4 g of carbohydrate. That is roughly 75 to 80 percent of calories from protein. A double scoop gives 48 g of protein for about 240 to 260 calories, which suits bigger lifters who want a full post-workout dose in one shake.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL IN ONE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">24 g protein, 5.5 g BCAAs, about 120-130 calories</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Per the label and ON's site at the time of writing, varying by flavor. The protein is an isolate-led whey blend, and ON describes it as banned-substance tested.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The rest of the label is typical of a flavored mass-market whey: cocoa in chocolate flavors, natural and artificial flavors, lecithin to help it mix, a gum for texture, and sweetening with sucralose and acesulfame potassium in most flavors. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, Gold Standard is not your powder. If you do not, the formula is straightforward and has been stable for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 5.5 g BCAA figure includes about 2.5 to 3 g of leucine per scoop in most whey of this protein density, the amino acid most closely tied to muscle protein synthesis. Combined with 24 g of protein, one scoop sits comfortably in the 20 to 40 g per sitting range that research on muscle building typically uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey is one of the most studied protein sources and is safe for most healthy adults at normal intakes. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication or have been advised to limit protein, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian first. This is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

