        <div id="isolate-hydro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gold Standard Isolate vs Platinum Hydrowhey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard 100% Isolate is ON's answer to people who want a leaner whey. Per ON's site at the time of writing, a serving gives 25 g of protein, 5.5 g of naturally occurring BCAAs, about 1 g of carbohydrate and 0.5 g of fat, with roughly 100 to 110 calories depending on flavor. It is ultra-filtered whey isolate, comes in Chocolate Bliss and Rich Vanilla, and is banned-substance tested per ON.
          </p>

          <p>
            The main reason to buy it is lactose. Isolate is filtered to remove most lactose, so people who get bloated on regular whey often tolerate it better. The second reason is calories: if you are cutting hard, saving 15 to 20 calories per scoop over Gold Standard Whey adds up over several shakes a day. The cost is about $2.35 per 25 g, per ON's listed price, and only two flavors. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains when that is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Platinum Hydrowhey is the premium tier. Per ON's site, each serving gives 30 g of protein from hydrolyzed whey protein isolate alone, with 8.8 g of BCAAs, which includes added BCAAs on top of those naturally present. Hydrolyzed protein is partly broken down, which may help some people with sensitive stomachs, and it digests quickly. Flavors are Turbo Chocolate, Velocity Vanilla and Supercharged Strawberry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydrowhey's problem is price. At about $74 for 20 servings on ON's site, it is roughly $3.10 per 25 g, more than twice Gold Standard Whey. The evidence that hydrolyzed whey builds more muscle than regular whey at the same protein dose is weak. Buy it only if regular whey and isolate both upset your stomach, or if you simply prefer it and do not mind paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="casein-plant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gold Standard Casein and Gold Standard Plant
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard 100% Casein gives 24 g of micellar casein per scoop, per ON, at about 120 calories per the label, in Chocolate Supreme, Creamy Vanilla, Cookies and Cream and Chocolate Peanut Butter. Casein digests slowly, which makes it a common choice for a pre-bed shake or a long gap between meals. Mixed with less liquid it thickens into a pudding, which buyers often use as a dessert.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is optional. If you already hit your daily protein target, there is no need for a second tub. Research on pre-sleep protein suggests a modest benefit for overnight muscle protein synthesis in people who train, but total daily protein matters far more. At about $1.90 per 25 g on ON's site, Casein is still reasonably priced, cheaper per gram than ON's isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gold Standard 100% Plant gives 24 g of protein per serving from pea, rice and fava bean proteins, per ON's site, with 0 g of sugar and no artificial colors, flavors or sweeteners. It comes in Creamy Vanilla and Rich Chocolate Fudge. The pea-rice-fava blend gives a complete amino acid profile, and avoiding artificial sweeteners is a plus for people who dislike sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is cost and range. At about $28 for 12 servings on ON's site, it is roughly $2.45 per 25 g, and only two flavors. For vegans that is acceptable, but plant-based buyers have strong alternatives with sport certification. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> rankings compare it with them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            ON describes Gold Standard Whey, Isolate, Platinum Hydrowhey and Casein as banned-substance tested on its US product pages at the time of writing. Optimum Nutrition has also long promoted Informed Choice certification on many Gold Standard flavors. Informed Choice tests finished products for substances banned in sport and lists certified products in a public database.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important detail is that certifications are product- and flavor-specific. A certification on Double Rich Chocolate in one market does not automatically cover every flavor or size, and certification status can change. If you are a drug-tested athlete, look up your exact product, flavor and lot number in the certifier's database before you buy, rather than relying on a logo in a listing photo.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, ON's scale, retail distribution and testing claims are reasonable reassurance. Whey is also less prone to heavy-metal issues than many plant proteins. If you buy Gold Standard Plant and drink it daily, the usual advice for plant proteins applies: vanilla tends to carry less cadmium and lead than chocolate, because cacao is itself a source. Our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> has more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Counterfeit Gold Standard has been reported over the years because it is such a popular product. Buy from Amazon itself, ON's official Amazon store or authorized retailers, check that the seal is intact, and compare the lot number and best-by date with what you would expect. Prices that look far too good from an unknown seller are a warning sign.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. ON vs Ghost, Premier, Dymatize and Myprotein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against Ghost Whey, Gold Standard is the value pick. Ghost gives 25 g of protein per scoop with a fully disclosed protein split and licensed cereal and dessert flavors, but costs roughly 30 to 50 percent more per gram, and we could not find a sport certification for it. If flavor is what keeps you drinking shakes, Ghost may be worth it; our sibling <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a> covers it in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Premier Protein powder, the comparison is closer. Premier gives 30 g of protein for about 150 calories per two-scoop serving at a similar or slightly lower cost per gram than Gold Standard's smaller tubs, but in just three flavors and without a sport certification we could find. Our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> has the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Dymatize ISO100, ON's Isolate is the direct rival. Both give about 25 g of protein in roughly 100 to 120 calories per scoop. Dymatize states ISO100 carries Informed Choice certification and offers many more flavors, while ON's Isolate has only two flavors, so for most isolate buyers ISO100 is the more flexible pick. Our sibling <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> compares them in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against budget brands like Myprotein Impact Whey, Gold Standard costs more per gram outside of sales, but offers more protein per scoop and an isolate-led blend. For people who buy one tub a month for years, the difference is small. For people on a very tight budget, Myprotein on sale is usually cheaper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy ON and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Gold Standard 100% Whey if you want one reliable whey that does everything reasonably well, you tolerate dairy, you do not mind sucralose, and you want to buy in a big tub at a good price. That covers most beginners, most gym-goers and most people adding a daily shake to hit a protein target. The 5 lb tub in Double Rich Chocolate is the classic first purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Gold Standard Isolate if regular whey makes you bloated or you want the leanest macros, Casein if you want a slow pre-bed shake or a protein pudding, and Plant if you are vegan and want a familiar brand without artificial sweeteners. Buy Hydrowhey only if nothing else agrees with your stomach and price is not a concern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip ON if you avoid artificial sweeteners and flavors, since most of the whey range uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium; if you want a grass-fed or single-ingredient whey, since ON does not market either; or if you want the most exciting flavors, which Ghost and other flavor-led brands do better. Our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide covers simpler labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drug-tested athletes can use ON, but only after checking the exact product and flavor in the Informed Choice database. If your flavor is not listed, choose one that is, or pick a different certified product.
          </p>
        </div>

